meta {
  name: Webhook
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{baseURL}}/google-calendar/webhook/
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Goog-Channel-ID: {{gcal_webhook_channel_id}}
  X-Goog-Resource-ID: {{gcal_webhook_resource_id}}
  X-Goog-Resource-State: exists
}

docs {
  Google's own push notification callback - Google POSTs here with no body,
  just headers, whenever a watched personal calendar changes (see
  PersonalConnect.bru + Sync.bru). Not something the frontend or a human
  caller ever hits directly; this request exists to manually replay a
  notification while testing.

  X-Goog-Channel-ID / X-Goog-Resource-ID must match a GoogleCalendarConnection
  row's watch_channel_id/watch_resource_id (set automatically after a
  successful personal connect, or self-healed on the next POST /sync/) -
  these are generated fresh per connection, so pull the current values from
  the DB (or from server logs) before running this manually. A
  mismatched/unknown channel is logged and ignored, not rejected - Google
  retries with backoff and can eventually disable a channel that keeps
  getting non-2xx, so this endpoint always answers 200.

  X-Goog-Resource-State: "sync" is Google's one-time handshake right after
  the channel is created (no-op, nothing to fetch yet); any other value
  (e.g. "exists") means "something changed, go re-fetch" and triggers the
  same sync_google_events_to_availability_blocks() that POST /sync/ calls
  manually - see Sync.bru for what that does to AssociateAvailabilityBlock.

  Method: POST
  Path: /google-calendar/webhook/
  Source: integrations.google_calendar.views.GoogleCalendarWebhookView
  Auth: None (Google calling, not an authenticated frontend request) - verified via channel id/resource id match instead.

  Response (always 200, regardless of outcome): {"detail": "Google Calendar webhook processed.", "data": null}
}
